.TH NICE 1 
.SH NAME
nice \- run a command at low priority
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nice
[
.BI \- number
]
command [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nice
executes
.I command
with low scheduling priority.
If the
.I number
argument is present, the priority is incremented (higher
numbers mean lower priorities) by that amount.
The default
.I number
is 10.
Note: v7's page said the increment was limited to 20.
The kernel clamps the resulting priority to the range 0 through 39
(0 through 2*NZERO\-1), not the increment;
a nice value already at 39 does not move.
.PP
The super-user may run commands with
priority higher than normal
by using a negative priority,
e.g. `\-\-10'.
A negative
.I number
from anyone else is silently taken as 0 \- the command still runs,
at the priority its caller was entitled to.
.PP
Note: v7's page also described
.IR nohup ,
a shell script that is not on this system.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
nice(2), ps(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Nice
returns the exit status of the subject command.
